.TH TOWERD 1 "" "tower @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
towerd \- the Tower daemon: guard proxy, geo check, usage and agent monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B towerd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B towerd
runs Tower's single background process: a local HTTP CONNECT proxy for Claude
Code, the multi-source location check, the network-health probe, the plan-usage
reader, and the Claude Code agent monitor. It owns all state; the dashboard is
a thin reader.
.PP
You do not normally start it by hand.
.BR tower (1)
starts it on demand, and the packaged systemd user unit starts it at login:
.PP
.RS
.B systemctl --user enable --now tower.service
.RE
.PP
One daemon per user, enforced by an
.BR flock (2)
on
.IR ~/.tower/daemon.lock ;
a second invocation prints
.I towerd: already running
and exits.
.SH ROUTING
Routing is installed by writing
.BR HTTPS_PROXY / HTTP_PROXY
(plus Claude's retry-budget variables) into the
.B env
block of
.IR ~/.claude/settings.json ,
and nowhere else — never a shell alias. It is removed again on every clean
exit, including on
.BR SIGTERM ,
so a stopped Tower always leaves Claude Code on a working direct connection.
.PP
A Claude Code session captures the proxy address once at launch, so the daemon
keeps the same port across restarts (remembered in
.IR ~/.tower/config.json ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TOWER_PORT
preferred proxy port (default 8888; the daemon picks a nearby free one if it
is taken)
.SH LINUX NOTES
Keep-awake uses
.BR systemd-inhibit (1),
which covers idle, sleep and the lid switch with no administrator password —
so the lid-closed mode that costs a password on macOS is free here. Reading
another process's working directory and finding which processes are connected
to the guard both go through
.IR /proc ,
so
.B lsof
is not needed. Focusing an agent's terminal tab works only under
.BR tmux ;
elsewhere the dashboard offers the
.B claude --resume
command instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tower (1),
.BR systemd-inhibit (1)
